{"uuid":"79470d6e-60dc-4834-af78-c8693163bd0e","parentUuid":"4b087c1d-a75d-44fa-91b8-6f379f296647","sessionId":"f90bcf50-d817-54c8-a6c1-bab525573534","timestamp":"2026-10-12T04:20:45.263Z","type":"system","provenance":"system","cwd":"/root/wt-a","version":"0.25.1","gitBranch":"master","subtype":"ui_telemetry","systemPayload":{"uiEvent":{"event.name":"qwen-code.api_response","event.timestamp":"2026-10-12T04:20:45.263Z","response_id":"924b3f30-9360-4d94-a54a-870e7a28e28b","execution_id":"c2c636d5-469e-4631-bec7-735df03ee606","model":"deepseek-flash","status_code":200,"duration_ms":3785,"input_token_count":14543,"output_token_count":564,"cached_content_token_count":13696,"thoughts_token_count":388,"total_token_count":15107,"response_text":"Main failure mode: with a fixed 3 attempts and no delay, all retries land in the same tight window, so any *correlated* failure — a 429 rate limit, an overloaded upstream, a dependency still mid-restart — is still true on attempt 2 and 3; the retries add load, amplify the overload, and lose to the same error while adding latency, and synchronized clients produce a thundering herd. Minimal fix: wrap the loop in exponential backoff with jitter (e.g. ~200ms base doubling per attempt, ±random jitter, capped), and retry only retryable/idempotent failures (5xx, timeouts, 429 honoring `Retry-After`) rather than all errors — that's a few lines and removes both the self-inflicted load and the synchronized-retry risk.","prompt_id":"f90bcf50-d817-54c8-a6c1-bab525573534########1","auth_type":"openai","ttft_ms":479}}}
{"uuid":"9db1e68e-4f23-485c-8c97-8bebe9ef771d","parentUuid":"79470d6e-60dc-4834-af78-c8693163bd0e","sessionId":"f90bcf50-d817-54c8-a6c1-bab525573534","timestamp":"2026-10-12T04:20:45.263Z","type":"assistant","provenance":"assistant_output","cwd":"/root/wt-a","version":"0.25.1","gitBranch":"master","model":"deepseek-flash","message":{"role":"model","parts":[{"text":"The previous round r01 timed out (empty answer). Now r02 from peer B: \"Retries use a fixed 3 attempts with no backoff.\" Reply in one short paragraph: main failure mode and minimal fix.\n\nI should answer directly. One short paragraph. Let me give the review.\n\nMain failure mode: retrying immediately with no delay — on transient/throttling errors (429, overloaded service) all 3 attempts hammer the dependency in the same tight window, so correlated failure (server overloaded, rate limit still active) means the retries just amplify load and all fail; also retries non-idempotent ops without jitter causes thundering herd of synchronized clients.\n\nMinimal fix: add exponential backoff with jitter between attempts (e.g., base 100-200ms, doubling, ±jitter, cap), and only retry retryable/idempotent errors.\n\nKeep it to one short paragraph. Also note that the prior round timed out — but that's context. Keep brief.\n\nI could just reply. Also should I write the artifact/answer file? The workflow seems to involve rounds/ files and refs-a2a.jsonl. The request mentions \"r02\", but the files are r01. The answer for r01 is empty/TIMEOUT. Hmm, should I create r02 files? The task framing: \"Review request r02 (from peer B). ... Reply in one short paragraph\". It says reply. I think the main deliverable is the paragraph reply in chat. But there's a pattern of files. Given the previous A->B direction, this is B->A now. r02 would be direction B->A. However the artifacts shown are only r01. Maybe I should just reply in chat; the runtime may record it.\n\nGiven \"Answer briefly\" config, just reply.\n\nLet me write a concise paragraph.","thought":true},{"text":"Main failure mode: with a fixed 3 attempts and no delay, all retries land in the same tight window, so any *correlated* failure — a 429 rate limit, an overloaded upstream, a dependency still mid-restart — is still true on attempt 2 and 3; the retries add load, amplify the overload, and lose to the same error while adding latency, and synchronized clients produce a thundering herd. Minimal fix: wrap the loop in exponential backoff with jitter (e.g. ~200ms base doubling per attempt, ±random jitter, capped), and retry only retryable/idempotent failures (5xx, timeouts, 429 honoring `Retry-After`) rather than all errors — that's a few lines and removes both the self-inflicted load and the synchronized-retry risk."}]},"usageMetadata":{"promptTokenCount":14543,"candidatesTokenCount":564,"thoughtsTokenCount":388,"totalTokenCount":15107,"cachedContentTokenCount":13696},"contextWindowSize":1000000}
